# <<.Name>>, for an agent

Read `<<.Name>> describe --json` first. It is the whole surface — every command,
its flags and arguments, the screens, the palette and the glyph set — in one
call, generated from the same declaration the tool runs on. It cannot describe a
flag the tool does not have.

## Where things live

- `internal/engine` — the domain. Change what <<.Name>> knows about here.
  It must not import anything terminal-shaped; a test enforces that.
- `internal/tui` — the interface. Regions are named in `regions.go`; a click
  resolves to a region name and index, never a coordinate.
- `internal/cli` — the command tree. Adding a command here adds it to the CLI,
  to `describe --json`, and (given a `Target`) to a context menu. Do not add it
  in more than one place; there is only one place.

## Looking at the screens

You cannot see a terminal, but you can read one. Every screen has a golden in
`internal/tui/testdata` — a captured frame with the colour stripped — so
"what does this look like at 80 columns" is a file, not a question for a human.

```
make frames     # render every screen to an HTML page as well
```

The goldens are rendered from `fixture()` in `internal/tui/fixture_test.go`,
not from a live backend. To see a state that does not exist yet, add it to
`states()` in `capture_test.go` and rerun with `-update-goldens`; the list is
walked by the goldens, the 80-column run, the colour check and the capture, so
one entry gets you all four.

## Before you say you are done

```
make check
```

That is what CI runs. If a screen changed on purpose, `go test ./internal/tui -update-goldens`
and read the diff in the goldens — they are plain text, and a surprise in one is
the point of having them.

## Rules that will fail your change if you break them

- A colour must come from `Palette`, named by the ROLE it plays. A raw
  `lipgloss.Color("205")` fails, because it says what a colour IS instead of
  what it is FOR.
- A non-ASCII character must be in `Glyphs`. Add it deliberately, with the
  reason, or use ASCII.
- The engine imports the standard library and its own backend's SDK. Nothing
  else.
- Anything a mouse can do, a key must also do.
